---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import DevEncodeBinary from '@/components/tools/text/DevEncodeBinary.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="binary-translator">
  <DevEncodeBinary client:load kind="text" direction="encode" autoDetect />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>One box, both directions</h2>
    <p>
      Paste any text. If the input has only ones, zeros and whitespace, the binary translator decodes
      it as binary; it encodes all other input. If the guess is wrong (for example, when your message is
      the word <code>1010</code>), use the swap button.
    </p>

    <h2>A quick reference</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Character</th><th>Binary (8-bit)</th><th>Character</th><th>Binary (8-bit)</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><code>A</code></td><td>01000001</td><td><code>a</code></td><td>01100001</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>B</code></td><td>01000010</td><td><code>b</code></td><td>01100010</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>M</code></td><td>01001101</td><td><code>m</code></td><td>01101101</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>Z</code></td><td>01011010</td><td><code>z</code></td><td>01111010</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>0</code></td><td>00110000</td><td><code>9</code></td><td>00111001</td></tr>
          <tr><td>space</td><td>00100000</td><td><code>!</code></td><td>00100001</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Uppercase letters all start <code>010</code>, lowercase letters all start <code>011</code>, and
      digits all start <code>0011</code>. The last five bits give the position in the group. With this
      pattern, you can read short binary strings by eye.
    </p>

    <h2>Why computers use base 2 at all</h2>
    <p>
      A physical component can reliably hold two states (charged or not, high or low voltage,
      magnetised one way or the other), but not ten. The rest is convention: which bit patterns mean
      which characters, how many bits make a unit, and the order of the bytes. Binary is counting with
      two symbols instead of ten.
    </p>

    <h2>Some things binary is not</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        It is not encryption. Anyone can reverse it. If a puzzle uses binary as its
        &ldquo;code&rdquo;, the puzzle is a lookup, not a cipher.
      </li>
      <li>
        It is not machine code. Machine instructions are also binary, but with a completely different
        mapping. An executable decoded as text gives gibberish.
      </li>
      <li>
        It is not one universal standard. The same bits mean different characters in ASCII, EBCDIC and
        UTF-16, so the mapping must be agreed in advance.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Choosing the settings</h2>
    <p>
      Keep the character set on UTF-8 unless an exercise specifies 7-bit ASCII. Keep the separator on
      spaces for output that a person reads: binary without spaces is unreadable after about three
      characters and impossible to proofread. Set it to &ldquo;none&rdquo; only when a machine reads
      the output.
    </p>

    <h2>Puzzle and homework tips</h2>
    <p>
      If a decoded message is garbage, first count the digits: the total must divide by 8 (or 7). If it
      divides by neither, a digit was lost in transcription. If it divides by both and only one gives
      readable text, that is the correct group size. If the plaintext looks like more encoded data,
      such as hex characters or a Base64 string, the puzzle probably has more stages.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
